import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.C1oW5ckO.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Building","description":"The shared build tool, Turborepo setup, and runtime caveats","frontmatter":{"navigation":{"category":"Plugin development"},"title":"Building","description":"The shared build tool, Turborepo setup, and runtime caveats","editLink":"https://github.com/filiphsps/pumpkin-plugins/edit/master/docs/building.md"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guides/building.md","filePath":"docs/building.md","lastUpdated":1792189040000}`),a={name:`guides/building.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="building" tabindex="-1">Building <a class="header-anchor" href="#building" aria-label="Permalink to “Building”">​</a></h1><h2 id="the-build-tool" tabindex="-1">The build tool <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-build-tool" aria-label="Permalink to “The build tool”">​</a></h2><p>From the workspace root, run <code>pnpm build</code> to build all plugin packages or <code>pnpm build --filter=@pumpkin-plugins/&lt;folder&gt;</code> to build one. Each package uses <code>pumpkin-plugins-build</code> (<code>tools/build</code>) to turn a plugin into a <code>.wasm</code> component. Configure it with the <code>pumpkinPlugin</code> key in the plugin&#39;s <code>package.json</code>:</p><div class="language-json"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">json</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;pumpkinPlugin&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: { </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;entry&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;src/plugin.ts&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;output&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;build/my-plugin.wasm&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;wasi&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: [] }</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>engine</code> is optional and defaults to <code>static-hermes</code>. Set it to <code>quickjs</code> to build a QuickJS plugin; both engines are supported. Current plugin packages use the Static Hermes default. See the <a href="./../tools/static-hermes-runtime/README">Static Hermes Runtime guide</a> for toolchain setup and supported API calls. Development, integration and release builds use the configured engine. The build tool provisions Static Hermes&#39;s pinned WASI SDK and <code>wit-bindgen</code> under <code>.cache/hermes/</code> when needed, then prepares Hermes automatically when Static Hermes is selected.</p><p>It resolves the plugin&#39;s declared <code>@pumpkin-plugins/pumpkin-api</code> dependency or npm alias and fetches the WIT files from the subdirectory pinned by its <code>wit.lock.json</code> (the WIT ABI must match the Pumpkin server), then resolves esbuild and the selected engine. On first use, and whenever the lockfile contents change, Git fetches the pinned commit into <code>.cache/pumpkin-api-wit/&lt;lock-digest&gt;/</code>; existing cache entries need no network access. Set <code>PUMPKIN_PLUGINS_CACHE_DIR</code> to move that cache. Static Hermes uses its generated WASI C bindings and component linker. QuickJS uses the Wasmtime 48 CLI fork of <code>componentize-qjs</code>. The fork is needed because the upstream Wasmtime 47 CLI fails to link the WASI HTTP types used by plugins. The pinned release&#39;s own build script uses a componentizer that produces 55 MB plugins and can&#39;t run the WASI socket code.</p><p>Production bundles are minified. Builds for plugins inside this repository fail when a component from either engine exceeds 5 MiB (5,242,880 bytes), before replacing the existing <code>.wasm</code> output. When the published build package is used outside this repository, it warns above that threshold and still writes the component. Development bundles stay readable and are exempt from the check. Static Hermes dev components optimize native code for size while keeping Hermes source locations and native line tables for backtraces.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>pumpkin-plugins-build</code></td><td><code>build/&lt;plugin&gt;.wasm</code>, plus the latest combined WIT snapshot in <code>build/wit/</code> when WASI imports are added</td></tr><tr><td><code>pumpkin-plugins-build --types-only</code></td><td>Guest declarations in <code>build/types/bindings/</code> for <code>tsc</code> and your editor</td></tr><tr><td><code>pumpkin-plugins-build --parse-ts</code></td><td>Opts Static Hermes into its partial, unstable TypeScript parser; unavailable with QuickJS</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Run <code>pnpm build:parse-ts</code> to build the repository&#39;s plugin packages with that parser. For the dev server, run <code>pnpm dev -- --parse-ts</code> to use that task for the initial build and watch builds.</p><p>Static Hermes uses Hades garbage collection by default. Set <code>&quot;staticHermesGc&quot;: &quot;malloc&quot;</code> in the package&#39;s <code>pumpkinPlugin</code> configuration to use malloc-managed GC. This choice requires Static Hermes.</p><p>Set <code>PUMPKIN_PLUGINS_VERBOSE_BUILD=1</code> to include Static Hermes tool output and the selected <code>--wasi-imports</code>, <code>--wasi-methods</code>, <code>--api-methods</code> and <code>--event-types</code> for each component. Root builds normally hide cached task logs, so add <code>--force</code> to print the selections for every plugin:</p><div class="language-sh"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">PUMPKIN_PLUGINS_VERBOSE_BUILD</span><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">1</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> pnpm</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> build</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --force</span></span></code></pre></div><p>An omitted method or event filter means all available operations or event variants are retained.</p><p>Only declarations persist under <code>build/types/</code>, so <code>build</code> and <code>typecheck</code> can run in parallel without touching the same files or keeping a second WIT tree. When a plugin requests WASI imports, each build uses a private WIT snapshot while it runs, then publishes a copy to <code>build/wit/</code> for inspection. Type generation prepares its WIT in a temporary directory and removes it afterward. Run <code>pnpm typecheck</code> once after a fresh checkout so your editor finds the types. The shared <code>plugin-kit</code> package does the same for its own source, generating declarations for its filesystem imports in its ignored <code>build/types/</code> directory.</p><p>The API package ships <code>wit.lock.json</code>, not a vendored WIT tree. The build command generates guest declarations from the fetched source into <code>build/types/bindings/</code>; a plugin&#39;s <code>tsconfig.json</code> includes those declarations before its source files and preserves WIT <code>u64</code>/<code>s64</code> values as <code>bigint</code>.</p><p>The <a href="./../tools/static-hermes-runtime/README">Static Hermes Runtime guide</a> lists the generated Node, pnpm and host-tool versions used by packed consumer builds. Set <code>STATIC_HERMES_CACHE_DIR</code> to move Hermes source and build artifacts outside the default workspace cache. Run <code>pnpm run test:external-consumer</code> to pack the public tool packages and their workspace dependencies, verify the packed public metadata and starter dependency ranges, install them from a temporary registry, and typecheck/build both generated standalone starters from a temporary consumer.</p><h2 id="runtime-caveats" tabindex="-1">Runtime caveats <a class="header-anchor" href="#runtime-caveats" aria-label="Permalink to “Runtime caveats”">​</a></h2><h3 id="quickjs-runtime" tabindex="-1">QuickJS runtime <a class="header-anchor" href="#quickjs-runtime" aria-label="Permalink to “QuickJS runtime”">​</a></h3><p>QuickJS plugins run inside the server&#39;s WebAssembly runtime:</p><ul><li>There is no global <code>TextEncoder</code>, <code>TextDecoder</code> or <code>fetch</code>. <code>fflate</code> provides <code>strToU8</code> and <code>strFromU8</code>. Plugins that need HTTP can import <code>wasi:http</code> by declaring the <code>http</code> capability; the update-check tool already does this.</li><li>WIT <code>u64</code>/<code>s64</code> values are JavaScript <code>bigint</code> in both directions. Keep generated <code>bigint</code> types; convert explicitly to <code>number</code> only when the value is within JavaScript&#39;s safe integer range.</li><li>Scheduler delays and periods are <code>u64</code> (<code>bigint</code>); handler and task IDs are <code>u32</code> (<code>number</code>). The plugin-kit scheduler helpers accept safe integer tick counts and pass them as <code>BigInt</code>.</li><li>Plugins that request permissions are prompted for on the server console the first time they load. Hot reload can&#39;t prompt and denies them, so the first load needs a restart.</li><li>Two builds of identical source produce different bytes. Anything that signs or uploads a build must use the artifact from the build job, never a rebuild.</li></ul><h3 id="static-hermes" tabindex="-1">Static Hermes <a class="header-anchor" href="#static-hermes" aria-label="Permalink to “Static Hermes”">​</a></h3><p>Static Hermes plugins use the WASI 2 C API path in <code>tools/static-hermes-runtime</code>. Its native JSI shim currently implements only part of the Pumpkin API. The runtime consumes the prepared WIT directory from <code>@pumpkin-plugins/build</code>, which owns the WASI version and WIT files. It validates every operation in imported WASI interfaces, including operations the plugin does not currently use, and generates native adapters for the operations selected by build analysis. The imported WASI 0.2.3 operations cover filesystem, HTTP, streams, clocks, TCP, UDP and sockets. Feature-gated operations enable their WIT feature when selected. Event converters keep complete payload fields for statically registered event types; dynamic or unresolved registrations retain every event case and payload field. Static Hermes development builds disable the shared automatic update check. WASI 3 support is deferred.</p><h2 id="turborepo" tabindex="-1">Turborepo <a class="header-anchor" href="#turborepo" aria-label="Permalink to “Turborepo”">​</a></h2><p>Tasks are defined in <code>turbo.json</code> and run with the root scripts (<code>pnpm build</code>, <code>pnpm typecheck</code>, <code>pnpm test</code>, <code>pnpm test:integration</code>, <code>pnpm readme</code>, <code>pnpm package</code>):</p><p>The <a href="/pumpkin-plugins/guides/turborepo">Turborepo commands guide</a> explains the <code>pnpm turbo</code> wrapper, its log defaults, and how to override them.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>transit</code></td><td>Does nothing; it carries a package&#39;s dependencies&#39; hashes (see below)</td></tr><tr><td><code>build</code></td><td>Caches <code>build/**</code> except <code>build/types/**</code>, plus package <code>dist/**</code> output</td></tr><tr><td><code>benchmark:build</code></td><td>Caches the engine-comparison components and stack-guard fixtures, with separate output paths for each configuration</td></tr><tr><td><code>typecheck</code></td><td>Caches the generated <code>build/types/**</code>, so a cache hit restores them</td></tr><tr><td><code>test</code></td><td>Caches <code>dist/**</code> for the <code>static-hermes-runtime</code> package&#39;s test build</td></tr><tr><td><code>test:integration</code></td><td>Depends on <code>build</code>, never cached (it starts a real server)</td></tr><tr><td><code>readme</code>, <code>readme:check</code></td><td>Never cached, they write or inspect files</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The <code>@pumpkin-plugins/build</code> test tasks also build <code>@pumpkin-plugins/static-hermes-runtime</code> first because the build tool imports its compiled package entry. Both public tools declare their <code>dist/**</code> output so Turbo restores the compiled files when those builds come from cache.</p><p>Run <code>pnpm clean</code> to remove workspace <code>node_modules</code>, root and workspace <code>build/</code> and <code>dist/</code> output, and <code>.turbo/cache/</code>. It preserves <code>.cache/</code>.</p><p><code>pnpm build</code> checks the selected Turbo task graph and prepares Static Hermes once before plugin builds start. QuickJS-only filters skip that setup. Direct package builds still prepare Hermes when needed.</p><h2 id="run-all-plugins-on-pumpkin" tabindex="-1">Run all plugins on Pumpkin <a class="header-anchor" href="#run-all-plugins-on-pumpkin" aria-label="Permalink to “Run all plugins on Pumpkin”">​</a></h2><p>Run <code>pnpm dev</code> to build every plugin and start the latest stable Pumpkin release. Run <code>pnpm dev --nightly</code> or <code>pnpm dev:nightly</code> to use Pumpkin&#39;s latest nightly build instead. Stable release metadata is cached in <code>.cache/pumpkin</code> for 24 hours; set <code>PUMPKIN_REFRESH_RELEASE=1</code> to force a one-run refresh. Nightly metadata refreshes on every start because the <code>nightly</code> tag is reused, and the binary cache uses the uploaded asset ID to pick up replaced builds. Stable binaries are checked against <code>checksums.sha256</code>; nightly binaries are checked against their GitHub release asset SHA-256 digest.</p><p>Pass <code>--release</code> to build the plugins with release settings while running the dev server. For example, <code>pnpm dev -- --release</code> uses release settings for both the initial build and hot-reload rebuilds.</p><p>Run <code>pnpm dev:no-hot-reload</code> to build once and start the same server with plugin hot reload and build watchers disabled. Add <code>--nightly</code> or run <code>pnpm dev:nightly:no-hot-reload</code> to use the latest nightly build. Run <code>pnpm dev:no-hot-reload -- --release</code> to build plugins with release settings. Restart the command after changing a plugin.</p><p>The dev server lives in <code>.cache/pumpkin-dev</code>, so its world and settings remain between runs. Each command sets Pumpkin&#39;s plugin hot reload to match the selected mode. In hot-reload mode, <code>pnpm dev</code> watches workspace packages, rebuilds affected plugins and copies each completed <code>.wasm</code> into the server&#39;s <code>plugins/</code> directory. This copy is needed because Pumpkin watches that directory, while build outputs live under each package. A file linked to a build output would not notify Pumpkin when the target changes. Automatic Market update checks are disabled for the default dev builds and included in <code>--release</code> builds. Reloading plugins that request permissions still requires granting permissions on their initial load.</p><p>Set <code>PUMPKIN_BIN</code> to use a local server binary instead of downloading the latest release. Set <code>PUMPKIN_CACHE_DIR</code> to change the binary cache directory. To reset the dev server&#39;s world, config and plugins, stop the server and remove <code>.cache/pumpkin-dev</code>.</p><p>The compiled build executable lives at <code>tools/build/dist/cli.js</code>. Turbo builds it and its Static Hermes runtime dependency before plugin builds or typechecks. For direct package commands after a fresh checkout, run <code>pnpm --filter @pumpkin-plugins/static-hermes-runtime build</code> and <code>pnpm --filter @pumpkin-plugins/build build</code> first.</p><p>An interactive invocation may show a cached notice when a newer <code>@pumpkin-plugins/build</code> release is available on npm. Help and version commands, CI, development mode (<code>PUMPKIN_DEV_MODE=1</code>) and non-interactive output skip the lookup; registry failures do not affect the build. Use <code>--no-update-notifier</code> or set <code>PUMPKIN_PLUGINS_NO_UPDATE_NOTIFIER=1</code> to disable notices. Preview a suggested migration from the plugin root with <code>pnpm dlx @pumpkin-plugins/codemod update-build .</code>.</p><p>The build tool, signing tool, plugin creator and test harness <code>typecheck</code> tasks run <code>tsc --noEmit</code>, so their package-level Turbo configurations declare no outputs. Plugin typechecks keep the root <code>build/types/**</code> outputs because they generate guest declarations needed by builds and editors.</p><p>Things to know when changing <code>turbo.json</code>:</p><ul><li><strong>Cache invalidation.</strong> The workspace packages are used as source (their <code>exports</code> point at <code>src/</code>), so nothing is built in between and Turborepo has no reason to look at a dependency&#39;s files. The <code>transit</code> task is the fix from the Turborepo docs (&quot;transit nodes&quot;): it has no script and depends on <code>^transit</code>, and <code>build</code>, <code>typecheck</code> and <code>test</code> depend on it, so a change in any package a package depends on (including <code>devDependencies</code> such as <code>tools/build</code>, whose <code>wasi-wit.lock.json</code> and <code>src/</code> decide what a plugin is built from) invalidates exactly its dependents. New tools need nothing added, as long as a plugin lists them in its <code>package.json</code>. <code>globalDependencies</code> is only for inputs outside any package: <code>.node-version</code> (the Node version selected in CI; Turborepo does not hash <code>process.version</code>), <code>tsconfig.base.json</code> and <code>pnpm-workspace.yaml</code> (the API package and ABI pin). The lockfile and the root <code>package.json</code> invalidate everything, and so do the packages the root <code>package.json</code> depends on (<code>tools/docs</code> and <code>tools/signing</code>), because Turborepo treats those as global.</li><li><strong>Environment variables.</strong> Turborepo runs tasks in strict env mode, so a variable a task reads has to be declared, or it never reaches the task. The <code>PUMPKIN_*</code> variables the test harness and the build&#39;s download cache read are in <code>globalPassThroughEnv</code>; they don&#39;t affect cache keys, which is fine because they only say where things are or what to run in uncached tasks. <code>TMPDIR</code> isn&#39;t passed through, so tests run under Turborepo create their temp directories in <code>/tmp</code>. Static Hermes toolchain paths pass through to the uncached <code>test:integration</code> task because integration fixtures can build plugins and should reuse the prepared CI toolchain.</li><li><strong><code>^</code> dependencies need a task the root doesn&#39;t have.</strong> The root package also defines a <code>build</code> script (<code>&quot;build&quot;: &quot;node scripts/build.mjs&quot;</code>), so <code>dependsOn: [&quot;^build&quot;]</code> would make the root package a dependency of itself and fail or loop. <code>transit</code> is not a root script, which is why it can use <code>^transit</code>. Don&#39;t add a root script of that name. See the Turborepo docs page &quot;Missing root task in turbo.json&quot;.</li><li><strong>Linting runs outside Turborepo.</strong> <code>pnpm lint</code> calls Biome and ESLint directly. Both are fast, so caching them wouldn&#39;t gain anything.</li><li><strong><code>agentGuidance</code> is off.</strong> By default Turborepo writes an <code>AGENTS.md</code> when it detects an AI agent and re-adds it if it is deleted. <code>&quot;agentGuidance&quot;: false</code> is the opt-out.</li><li><strong>Package manager.</strong> Declared with <code>devEngines.packageManager</code> in the root <code>package.json</code>, which Turborepo, pnpm and <code>pnpm/action-setup</code> all read.</li><li><strong>Remote cache</strong> isn&#39;t configured. CI caches <code>.turbo</code> with <code>actions/cache</code>. Set <code>TURBO_TOKEN</code> and <code>TURBO_TEAM</code> to use a remote cache instead.</li></ul><p>The installed Turborepo ships its own docs for the exact version: <code>docs/</code> inside the <code>turbo</code> package (<code>node -p &quot;require(&#39;path&#39;).dirname(require.resolve(&#39;turbo/package.json&#39;))&quot;</code> from the repo root).</p>`,41)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};